    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so the bacteria basically make the environment more acidic, which then helps the yeast? i'm curious about the specific balance though. if the acidity gets too high or if the yeast doesn't produce enough CO2, does the whole culture just die off, or does it just become a different kind of starter?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The balance between acidity and yeast activity is crucial in sourdough. If the acidity becomes too high, it can inhibit yeast growth and lead to a decrease in CO2 production, causing the fermentation to slow down or become unbalanced. When this happens, the approximately 10,000-100,000 colony-forming units (CFU) per gram of mixture is disrupted, potentially causing the culture to shift favorably towards lactic acid production or a drastic imbalance between yeast and bacteria populations, rather than dying off completely. These imbalances can result in a loss of character in the sourdough or the rise of opportunistic microorganisms.",
